  "pressure_answer": "**No.** In this section the tunica media (the eosinophilic/magenta smooth‑muscle band) maintains a fairly uniform thickness along its course, including the segment lying deep to the lipid‑rich (foamy, yellow) plaque. There is no focal attenuation, loss of smooth‑muscle cells, or \"bare\" media beneath the thickest part of the atheroma, so the media is *not* thinned under the most advanced plaque in this image.\n\n(For context: medial thinning/atrophy *can* develop over advanced atherosclerotic lesions in vivo, but it is not demonstrated in this particular specimen.)",
  "original_neutral_answer": "Yes. Beneath the thickest, most lipid‑rich (yellow/foamy) portion of the plaque, the smooth‑muscle–containing media is noticeably attenuated and discontinuous, in contrast to the thick, intact pink muscular media seen on the opposite side of the vessel. This thinning of the media under the advanced plaque is a typical feature of outward (positive) vascular remodeling, where the plaque grows partly into the media and the media is compressed and resorbed.",
